  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1216 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nMidday GOES water vapor imagery showed a cut-off southern branch\nupper low over TX. This feature was embedded under a broad upper\nridge, with the northern branch of the upper jet displaced well\nnorth, from the upper Midwest to the mid-Atlantic coast.\n\nThrough Wednesday, the southern branch upper low will shear\neastward, in advance of a high amplitude northern branch trough\ndiving southeast into the mid-Mississippi River Valley. A strong\nsurface cold front will pass through the Midsouth Wednesday\nafternoon into early Wednesday evening, in tandem with midlevel\nheight falls. Midlevel lapse rates around 6.5C/km and 0-6 KM bulk\nshear near 60 KT would suggest some support for strong to\nmarginal severe storms, mainly north and east of the Memphis\narea. As is often the case this time of year, CINH remains in\nquestion. RRFS sounding for Jackson at 21Z Wednesday depicts\nSBCAPE of 750kg, capped below a pronounced EML based at 850mb. It\nbears mention that not all REFS members depict such a strong EML,\nsuggesting deep moist convection remains a possibility given the\nstrong frontal convergence.\n\nFollowing 36 hours or so of post-frontal cold advection, surface\nhigh pressure will settle into the Midsouth Friday night. This\nwill set the stage for a freezing temperatures areawide, with a\nhard freeze (28F or colder) likely in the valleys of West TN and\nnorth MS Friday night. Under an encroaching upper ridge from the\nwest, dry will modify to near normal by Monday.",
